    {
      "uri": "col:hversion/new_south_wales/history/272250b0aea1",
      "colony": "new_south_wales",
      "section_slug": "history",
      "version_hash": "272250b0aea1",
      "asserted_by": "Colonial Office List",
      "text": "This part of the Australian coast is said to have been discovered by Portuguese and Dutch navigators in the 17th century, but no settlement was made. Captain Cook explored it in 1770, and gave it the name of New South Wales, but the first settlement was not formed until 1788, when the convict station (intended for Botany Bay) was established at Port Jackson under Captain Phillips, R.N. The capital was named after Viscount Sydney, who was Secretary of State for the Colonies when the settlement was formed. The Settlement of New South Wales was then held to include the whole eastern portion of the continent. The first newspaper was established in 1803. The population in 1810 was 9,000. The Merino sheep was introduced in this year. The passage through the Blue Mountains, giving access to the interior plains, was not discovered until 1813. Moreton Bay was settled in 1824. In 1828 the total population was only 37,000. Convicts ceased to arrive in 1841. The discovery of gold in 1851 led to a very rapid growth of what is now Victoria, settled in 1836, which was made a separate Colony in 1855. Responsible government was established in 1855. Moreton Bay, to the north, was constituted a separate Colony in 1859, under the name of Queensland.",
      "edition_years": [
        1894,
